-- The freshness index that makes the subscription sweep a scan instead of a
-- table walk.
--
-- Generated by hand and reviewed by hand, like the other migrations in this
-- directory.
--
-- ## What this closes
--
-- `push_subscriptions.last_seen_at` is the only handle on how fresh a
-- registration is, and the sweep that prunes stale registrations has to read
-- it. Without an index the sweep walks every subscription in the table on
-- every run — and the table only ever grows, because a subscription is a
-- browser registration and browsers accumulate.
--
-- The index is on `last_seen_at` alone, and not on
-- `(business_id, last_seen_at)`, because the sweep is not tenant-scoped: it is
-- a table-wide lifecycle job that runs across every tenant at once, the same
-- way `idx_wallet_callback_deliveries_received` exists for the wallet's own
-- ageing sweep.
--
-- `NULL` is indexed in Postgres, so the "never delivered to" rows the sweep
-- also considers are covered by the same index.
--
-- ## What this migration does NOT do
--
-- It does not delete anything. The sweep that reads this index is the thing
-- that decides what is stale, and it is a separate, reviewable behaviour with
-- its own counts.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
CREATE INDEX "idx_push_subscriptions_last_seen" ON "push_subscriptions" USING btree ("last_seen_at");
